## A B S T R A C T
Due to the increasingly serious antibiotic-related pollution, it is crucial to develop novel green bioadsorbents to effectively remove antibiotics from aqueous solutions. In this study, Fe doped zeolitic imidazolate frameworks-8 loaded cellulose (Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose) aerogels were prepared. The synthesized Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose aerogels were characterized experimentally including morphology observation and chemical compositions determination. The effects of bioadsorbent dosage, solution pH, adsorption time, initial TC concentration and adsorption tem­ perature on the TC adsorption behaviors were systematically studied. Due to the introduction of Fe in the ZIF-8, the maximum adsorption capacity of Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose for TC could reach as high as $1359.2\mathrm{mg/g}$ , which is higher than the reported ZIF-8 loaded polysaccharide based adsorbents. The adsorption kinetics and isotherm of TC adsorption were also determined. With the cellulose as the matrix to load Fe/ZIF-8, the obtained Fe/ZIF$8@$ cellulose aerogels exhibited good reusability. Most importantly, the TC adsorption mechanism was pro­ posed. The results of our finding suggest that the Fe doping into MOFs is an effective strategy to improve the antibiotics adsorption performance and the application of cellulose as the matrix is a valuable method to increase the cyclic utilization. This study highlights the potentials of applying the Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose aerogels in the antibiotics removal for practical wastewater.
## 1. Introduction
With the rapid development of science and technology, the water con­ sumption in the fields of industry, agriculture and life is increasing, and correspondingly, the water pollution is becoming increasingly serious. Antibi­ otics are one kind of the most widespread and dangerous pollutants in the wastewater of clinical treatments and personal care products since there are no discharged restrictions for the antibiotics into the environment [1]. Besides, part of antibiotics is discharged into water due to their overuse, leading to environmental pollution. The abuse and misuse of antibiotics lead to the increased bacterial resistance. Moreover, antibiotics can form a complex contaminant co-existence system with other contaminants in wastewater, making them difficult to be removed. Therefore, effectively removing the re­ sidual antibiotics in water has become an urgent issue to be solved [2].
Many different methods have been reported to remove antibiotics from wastewater, including photodegradation [3], biodegradation [4], membrane filtration [5], electrochemical degradation [6] and adsorp­ tion [7]. Among them, adsorption is the most convenient and effective method because of its simple operation, high efficiency, and no pollution [8]. The choice of adsorbent is very important for the adsorption tech­ nique. The saturation adsorption capacity, the stability and regeneration properties become primary considerations when choosing the adsor­ bents. Traditional adsorbents including activated carbon, zeolite, and silica gel display some disadvantages, such as low adsorption capacity and low adsorption reuse performance, which are difficult to achieve desirable antibiotics removal effect.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as a kind of porous hybrid com­ pounds, are self-assembled through the coordination of metal ions and organic ligands. As new functional materials, MOFs show their potential applications in the areas of catalysis, energy storage, chemical sensing and drug delivery [9–11]. Recently, more and more attentions of MOFs have been received on the water treatment as adsorbents to remove inorganic and organic contaminations due to their three-dimensional crystal structures with large surface area, tunable high porosity, uni­ form pore size, numerous active metal sites, ease of functionalization [12–14]. Hierarchical porous zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) with meso- and macropores was developed and exhibited enhanced tetracycline hydrochloride [15] adsorption capacity due to its novel hierarchical porous structure [16]. A stable mesoporous MOF (MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr}){\cdot}S0_{3}\mathrm{H})$ was firstly explored to be used to remove the fluo­ roquinolone antibiotics from water and it exhibited high adsorption capacities and fast removal rates of norfloxacin, ofloxacin, and enoxacin [17]. Three Fe-based MOFs (Fe-MIL-101, Fe-MIL-100, and Fe-MIL-53) were synthesized through hydrothermal method, and the fabricated Fe-MIL-101 displayed the best removal behavior of tetracycline [18]. In order to achieve improved adsorption capacity, the construction of bimetallic MOF with a markedly synergistic effect was carried out. For example, Ni and $Z\mathrm{n}$ bimetallic MOF (Ni–Zn MOF) was prepared by a facile solvothermal approach and the achieved Ni–Zn MOF exhibited an enhanced adsorption rate and higher adsorption capacity towards Congo red than those of monometallic Ni MOF or Zn MOF [19]. Zhou et al. reported a highly stable bimetallic Zr–Co MOFs fabrication method and the as-synthesized materials exhibited extraordinary anti­ biotic cefradine adsorption performance [20]. Fe doped ZIF-67 was prepared by a simple way and it showed a great adsorption ability of $135.14~\mathrm{mg/g}$ for adsorption of rhodamine B from water [12].
Although MOFs as the adsorbents have been extensively studied in the treatment of wastewater, the adsorbents in the form of powders exhibited some limitations in the practical applications, such as fragility and poor processability which result in the complex separation and difficult recycling ability along with unavoidable material loss. These not only decrease the utilization of MOFs, but also even cause secondary pollution of the environment [21]. Therefore, MOFs loading into suit­ able matrix has been considered to be an ideal solution to solve the above problems. The aluminium-based MOF and reduced graphene oxide were incorporated into reinforced alginate beads and the prepared beads showed high tetracycline and ciprofloxacin removal capacities from aqueous environment [1]. ZIF-L/foaming gelatin aerogels with three-dimensional hierarchical porous structure were prepared through integrating ZIF-L with gelatin aerogels, and the obtained aerogels dis­ played a rapid and effective adsorption of TC [22].
Cellulose is a natural polysaccharide with abundant hydrophilic groups which is a sustainable biopolymer material with abundant sources on the earth. Natural cellulose exhibits wide applications in the form of beads, films, aerogels and resins [23]. Recently, cellulose based aerogels with the cellulose as the supporters show great potentials in the heavy metals and organic dyes adsorption applications, due to its high porosity, large specific surface area, biocompatibility, flexibility and renewability sustainability [24]. For example, cellulose grafted with PEI aerogel beads were developed for simultaneous and efficient adsorption of $\mathbf{Cr(VI)}$ in the presence of various interference substances [25]. Bac­ terial cellulose $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{ZIF}{-8}$ composite aerogels were prepared through ZIF-8 nanoparticles in-site growth into bacterial cellulose and they exhibited superior adsorption performance of heavy metal ions [26]. Jin et al. fabricated nanocrystalline cellulose-hydroxyapatite composites and the prepared cellulose based composites showed high chlortetracycline hydrochloride adsorption efficiency [27].
In this study, Fe doped ZIF-8 were firstly synthesized by in-situ growth and employed cellulose as the supporter to prepare bimetallic MOFs loaded cellulose aerogel, noted Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose aerogels. Tetracycline [15] is a broad-spectrum antibiotic which is commonly used to treat infections in the world due to their low production cost and high effectiveness [28]. So TC was chosen as an adsorbant model in our study. The Fe doping aims to construct bimetallic MOF (Fe/ZIF-8) nanocrystals which were expected to synergistically improve the adsorption capacity towards TC. The application of renewable cellulose as the MOF sup­ porter aiming to provide a three-dimensional interconnected structure could efficiently load the Fe/ZIF-8 nanocrystals, which successfully resolved the issues of hard-separation and difficult recycling of MOF powders. The chemical compositions of the as-prepared Fe/ZIF-8@cel­ lulose aerogels were characterized by several methods. The TC adsorp­ tion performance was carried out by batch adsorption experiments. Based on the above studies, the TC adsorption mechanism of Fe/ZIF$8@$ cellulose aerogels was proposed. The prepared Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose adsorbent exhibited excellent TC adsorption ability, easy-separation and good reusability. This study developed a novel and green bio­ adsorbent for TC efficient removal, which provided a new construction idea in the water treatment field.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Ferrous sulfate heptahydrate $\mathrm{(FeSO_{4}{\cdot}7H_{2}O}\mathrm{\cdot}$ , sodium hydroxide (NaOH, ${>}96.0~\%)$ , epichlorohydrin (ECH, ${>}99.5~\%$ were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Microcrystalline cellulose (MCC) and 2-methylimidazole were purchased from Shanghai Aladdin Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd. Zinc nitrate hexahydrate (Zn $(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ by Shanghai Jiuyi Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Methanol and urea were purchased from Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd. TC was purchased from Shanghai Ekear Bio@Tech Co. Ltd.
## 2.2. Synthesis of cellulose aerogels
Cellulose aerogels were synthesized according to a previously re­ ported method [29]. Briefly, $1_{\mathrm{~8~MCC}}$ powders were dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ pre-cooled NaOH/urea $(7~\mathrm{wt^{9}/0/12~w t^{9}/0})$ aqueous solution and mixed in an ice-water bath for 6 h to obtain a transparent cellulose solution. Then, 1 mL ECH $(5\mathrm{wt\%})$ was added into the cellulose solution and mixed well. Finally, the cellulose hydrogel was obtained by pouring the mixture into a mold and placing them into de-ionized water for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The cellulose aerogel was achieved after washing and freeze-drying at $-40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 days.
## 2.3. Synthesis of ZIF-8@cellulose and Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose aerogels
The ZIF-8@cellulose and Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose aerogels were synthe­ sized by a simple in situ growth method. Typically, $\mathrm{5~mmol}$ of FeS­ $\mathrm{\Delta_{O_{4}\cdot7H_{2}O}}$ and $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ were dissolved in $30~\mathrm{\mL}$ methanol solution with various molecular ratios (0:5 and 2:3). Then, $_{0.5~g}$ cellu­ lose aerogels were placed into the above solutions and stirred for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ , respectively. Subsequently, 20 mmol of 2-methylimidazole dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol was added to the above mixture and stirred for $^{6\mathrm{h}}$ . The mixture was placed in a vacuum drying oven at $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ to ensure the integrity of Fe/ZIF-8 nanocrystals [30]. Finally, the samples were washed with methanol solution for three times and dried in a freezedryer at $-40\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ for 2 days. The aerogels were noted as ZIF$8@$ cellulose and Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose, respectively. The preparation process of Fe/ZIF- $8@$ cellulose aerogels is presented in Scheme 1.
## 2.4. Characterization
The morphologies of the prepared aerogels were investigated by Scanning Electron Microscope (SEM, JSM-7600F, JEOL, Japan). X-ray energy dispersive spectroscope (EDS) and element mappings were car­ ried out as well. Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra of the ob­ tained specimen were recorded using a Spectrum Two spectrometer (Perkin Elmer, Akron, OH, USA) with the wavenumber range of $4000{\mathrm{-}}400{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ . X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were recorded with Thermo Escalab 250Xi instrument (Thermo Fisher, USA) using Al Ka radiation (1486.6 eV, 150 W) with the base pressure $<10^{-8}$ Torr.
## 2.5. Adsorption experiments
To prevent the photodegradation of TC, all TC adsorption experi­ ments were performed in the dark condition. The effects of bioadsorbent dosage $(0.1\mathrm{-}1.0\mathrm{g})$ , pH (1−12), initial TC concentration $(100-1000\mathrm{mg/}$ L), contact time $(0{-}720~\mathrm{min})$ and temperature $(15/55~^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ on the TC adsorption behaviors were systematically investigated to determine the
A. Liu et al.
adsorption properties of the Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose aerogels with respect to TC. The batch experiments were carried out at $150\mathrm{rpm}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . The pH of TC solution was regulated with 0.1 M HCl and NaOH. Supernatants were taken out at a certain time and their adsorption values were measured at $357~\mathrm{{nm}}$ using a UV-2450 UV/Vis spectrophotometer. The removal efficiency, the adsorption capacity $\left(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{t}}\right)$ , the equilibrium adsorption capacity $\left(\mathrm{Q_{e}}\right)$ were determined:
$$
Q_{t}(\mathrm{mg/g})=\frac{(C_{0}-C_{t})\times V}{W}
$$
$$
Q_{e}(\mathrm{mg/g)=\frac{(C_{0}-C_{e})\times V}{W}}
$$
Removal efficiency $(\%)=\frac{\left(C_{0}-C_{e}\right)}{C_{0}}\times100\%$
where $\mathrm{C}_{0},\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{t}}$ and ${\sf C}_{\mathrm{e}}$ are the initial, any time and equilibrium concen­ trations of TC (mg/L), $\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ and $\mathsf{w}$ are the solution volume (mL) and bio­ adsorbent dosage (mg).
## 2.6. Reusability of Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose aerogels
For the desorption/re-adsorption cycles, $50~\mathrm{mg}$ bioadsorbent was added into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ tetracycline solution $\mathrm{(50~mg/L)}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The TCadsorbed bioadsorbent was regenerated by immersing into $20~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol for $^\mathrm{~6~h~}$ to remove adsorbed TC molecules. Then, the bio­ adsorbent was reused in the adsorption experiments and the process was repeated for five times.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Construction of Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose aerogels
The morphology of the Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose aerogel was studied by SEM. As displayed in Fig. 1 (a-c), a three-dimensional structure with many macropores was exhibited, which favors the TC adsorption through the pore filling approach. As shown in Fig. 1b, Fe/ZIF-8 nano­ crystals were uniformly grown on the smooth inner walls of the mac­ ropores in situ. The loaded Fe/ZIF-8 nanocrystals with uniform size were shown in the higher magnification image (Fig. 1c), which is in line with the reported Fe doped ZIF-8 nanocrystals [31]. EDS analysis was carried out and the spectrum is shown in Fig. 1d. It can be found that the C, O, N, Fe and Zn elements existed in the bioadsorbent. The element mappings of them are presented in Fig. 2e-i. All of these five elements were uni­ formly distributed on the bioadsorbent, indicating that Fe/ZIF-8 nano­ crystals had been loaded into the cellulose aerogels. Combining the above results, the Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose aerogels with uniform Fe/ZIF-8 nanocrystals distribution were successfully prepared and which is in good agreement with the original construction of this study.
## 3.2. Characterization
XRD patterns of cellulose aerogel, Fe/ZIF-8 nanocrystals and Fe/ZIF$8@$ cellulose aerogel are presented in Fig. 2A. For cellulose aerogel (curve a), a dominant diffraction peak appeared at 2θ values of $20.08^{\circ}$ corresponding to (110) plane which is a typical cellulose II XRD pattern [32]. In the case of Fe@ZIF-8 nanocrystals, the obvious diffraction peaks were found at $18.03^{\circ}$ (222), $26.73^{\circ}$ (334), $29.47^{\circ}$ (244), which is consistent with the reported ZIF-8 crystal structure [33]. When Fe/ZIF-8 nanocrystals were loaded into cellulose aerogel (curve c), some peaks of Fe/ZIF-8 were still evident, illustrating the Fe/ZIF-8 nanocrystals exis­ ted in the cellulose aerogel.
FTIR analysis was carried out to determine changes in chemical composition of cellulose aerogels before and after loading with MOF. FTIR spectra of cellulose, ZIF-8@cellulose and Fe/ZIF- $\cdot8@$ cellulose aer­ ogels are shown in Fig. 2B. Cellulose (curve a) showed its characteristic peaks locating at 1160 and $1058~\mathrm{\cm}^{-1}$ (the C-O-C stretching), $3700{-}3000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (the -OH stretching), 2890 (the - ${\mathrm{.CH}}_{2}$ stretching) and $898~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (the $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ deformation mode of the glycosidic linkage) [34,35]. For ZIF- $\{\boldsymbol{8}\ @\$ cellulose, the new peaks appeared at $431~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (the $\mathrm{{Zn-N}}$ stretching), $760~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (the bending vibration mode of the imid­ azole ring), $1576~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{N}$ stretching vibration of imidazole) and $3120~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (imidazole C–H stretching), respectively, indicating the existence of ZIF-8 [22,36,37] (curve b). In the case of Fe/ZIF$8@$ cellulose, a new peak emerged at $610~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which is assigned to the Fe–O stretching [31], confirming Fe was doped into ZIF-8 and the Fe/ZIF-8 nanocrystals were successfully loaded into the cellulose aerogel.
The chemical states on the surface of Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose were determined by XPS. Fig. 2C displays the survey scan spectrum of Fe/ZIF$\{\boldsymbol{8}\ @\$ cellulose, proving the existence of the elemental compositions of C, O, N, Zn and Fe. The high-resolution C 1 s spectrum could be fitted to four curves with the peaks at 288.6, 287.2, 285.8 and $284.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ corre­ sponding to the $\mathtt{C=}0$ C–C, C–O and $\mathsf{C-N}$ of Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose, respectively (Fig. 2D). The Fe characteristic peaks (Fig. 2E) were observed in the form of two Fe valence states, including $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ The peaks with binding energies at $710.2\mathrm{eV}$ and $724.2\mathrm{eV}$ were assigned to Fe $\ensuremath{2\mathrm{p}}^{3/2}$ and Fe $\mathsf{2p}^{\mathsf{1}\bar{/2}}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , and the peaks locating at $714.1\mathrm{eV}$ and $725.3\mathrm{eV}$ were attributed to Fe $\ensuremath{2\mathrm{p}}^{3/2}$ and Fe $\ensuremath{2\mathrm{p}}^{1/2}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , respectively. The existence of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ demonstrated the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Fe}}^{0}$ went through a certain degree of oxidation in the process of synthesis, which is similar to reported result of Fe doped ZIF-8 [38]. There was two deconvolution peaks on the $\mathtt{Z n2p}$ spectrum (Fig. 2F) which located at $1021.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $1044.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ assigning to $\mathrm{Zn}2\mathrm{p}^{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Zn~}2\mathrm{p}^{1/2}$ , respectively [31].
## 3.3. Batch experiments
## 3.3.1. Effect of bioadsorbent dosage
The effect of bioadsorbent dosage on the TC adsorption capacity was investigated (Fig. 3A). It can be clearly seen that the equilibrium adsorption capacity decreased from $435~\mathrm{mg/g}$ to $87{\mathrm{~mg/g}}$ with the in­ crease of bioadsorbent dosage from 0.1 to $1.0~\mathrm{g/L}$ . This is because the number of effective adsorption sites increased with the bioadsorbent dosage increasing. At the same time, a competition existed between these adsorption sites for the TC adsorption. Therefore, the ability to make full use of these adsorption sites gradually decreased with the presence of additional unoccupied sites, resulting in a smaller equilib­ rium adsorption capacity for higher bioadsorbent dosage [38]. Howev­ er, the removal efficiency of TC increased from $44\%$ to $87\%$ with the increase of the bioadsorbent dosage. This is also due to the number of effective adsorption sites increased with the increase of bioadsorbent dosage, bringing about the increase of the TC removal efficiency.
Combining the equilibrium adsorption capacity and the removal effi­ ciency, $0.3\mathrm{g/L}$ of bioadsorbent was selected or the following adsorption experiments.
## 3.3.2. Effect of solution pH
The solution pH is one of the important parameters which affect the adsorption capacity of the adsorbate in an aqueous solution because it directly affects the surface charge of the adsorbent and the ionizing degree of the adsorbate [31]. TC displays three different ionic species in an aqueous solution which are in the forms of cationic $\mathrm{TC^{+}}$ , zwitterionic ${\mathrm{TC}}^{0}$ and anionic $\mathrm{TC^{-}}/\mathrm{TC}^{2-}$ based on its dissociation constant (pKa of 3.3, 7.7 and 9.7) [39]. The experiment was carried out by using $30~\mathrm{mg}$ bioadsorbents into $100\mathrm{mL}$ TC solutions with concentration of $100\mathrm{mg/L}$ with $\mathsf{p H}$ ranging from 2 to 12. The effect of solution pH on the TC adsorption capacity and the Zeta potentials of bioadsorbent are dis­ played in Fig. 3B. The equilibrium adsorption capacity of TC sharply increased from 21.4 to $220.3\mathrm{mg/g}$ with the increase of solution pH from 1 to 5, and then falled down to $58.9\mathrm{\mg/g}$ with the solution pH decreased to 12. As shown in Fig. 3B, when the solution pHwas ${<}5$ , the Zeta potential of bioadsorbent was positive. For TC, it exhibited a pos­ itive charge when $1<\mathrm{pH}<3.3$ due to the deprotonation of TC. Thus, electrostatic repulsion dominated between TC molecule and the bio­ adsorbent, which was not benefit for the TC adsorption. With the solu­ tion pH increasing, the positive charge of bioadsorbent gradually decreased, while TC became a zwitterion when $3.3~<~\mathrm{pH}~<~4.6.$ Therefore, the electrostatic repulsion was attenuated by degrees and the adsorption capacity of TC was highly enhanced. In the pH range of 4.6–12, TC changed its form gradually from zwitterion to anion and the surface of bioadsorbent became negatively charge. Thus, the electro­ static repulsion increased between TC molecule and the bioadsorbent, leading to the gradual reduced the equilibrium adsorption capacity of
TC. Therefore, electrostatic interaction was confirmed to play an essential part in TC adsorption behavior of the prepared bioadsorbent.
## 3.3.3. Adsorption kinetics
The effect of adsorption time on the TC adsorption capacity was studied and the result is shown in Fig. 4A. A quick adsorption behavior of TC was exhibited within $20\mathrm{min}$ , then a slow TC adsorption phase was displayed until $540~\mathrm{min}$ and finally the adsorption process reached the equilibrium phase at $600\ \mathrm{min}$ with the equilibrium TC adsorption ca­ pacity of $216.9\mathrm{mg/g}$ . This phenomenon could be due to the 3D porous structure of the bioadsorbent with large amount of active adsorption sites at first, then the effective adsorption sites became fewer and fewer until they were all occupied.
The adsorption kinetic of TC adsorbed on the bioadsorbent was explored by fitting with pseudo-first-order kinetics, pseudo-secondorder kinetics and intra-particle diffusion models.
Pseudo-first-order kinetic model:
$$
l n(Q_{e}-Q_{t})=l n Q_{e}-\mathbf{k}_{1}t
$$
Pseudo-second-order kinetic model:
$$
t\bigg/Q_{t}=\frac{1}{\mathbf{k}_{2}{Q_{e}}^{2}}+\frac{t}{Q_{e}}
$$
Intra-particle diffusion model:
$$
Q_{t}=\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{i}}t^{1/2}+\mathbf{C}
$$
where $\mathbf{k}_{1}$ is the rate constant $(\mathbf{h}^{-1})$ , $\mathbf{k}_{2}$ is the rate constant $(\mathbf{g}{\cdot}\mathbf{m}\mathbf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathbf{h}^{-1})_{:}$ , $\bf{k}_{i}$ is the intra-particle diffusion rate constant, $\mathrm{mg/(g{\cdot}h^{1/2}})$ , C presents the thickness of the boundary layer.
The plots of $\ln(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{e}^{-}}\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{t}})$ vs. time is shown in Fig. 4B, and its slope and intercept of the fitted line presents the adsorption rate constant $(\mathbf{k}_{1})$ and the equilibrium adsorption $\left(\mathrm{Q_{e}}\right)$ . And the plots of $\mathrm{{\sft}/Q_{\mathrm{t}}}$ vs. time and the fitted line are displayed in Fig. 4C. The calculated kinetics parameters for TC adsorption by the bioadsorbent are presented in Table 1. It can be clearly found that the pseudo-second-order kinetic model with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{\mathrm{R}}}^{2}$ of 0.995 exhibited a better fit than the pseudo-first-order model with ${\mathrm{R}}^{2}$ of 0.971. The pseudo-second-order kinetic model assumes the adsorption process largely belongs to the chemisorption, demonstrating the chem­ ical adsorption was the essence for the TC adsorption on the bio­ adsorbent [40].
The intra-particle diffusion model was applied to further understand the rate controlling steps of the adsorption process. The plots of $\mathbf{Q}_{\mathrm{t}}$ vs $\mathbf{t}^{1/2}$ are illustrated in Fig. 4D. There were three stages in the fitted data. The first stage displayed a fast and external TC adsorption process that TC molecules diffused from the boundary layer to the surface of bio­ adsorbent. The second stage exhibited an intra-particle diffusion process that the TC molecules diffused from the surface into internal pores of the bioadsorbent. The third stage was the adsorption equilibrium process that the TC molecules slowly diffused into the bioadsorbent until the adsorption reached to the equilibrium [41,42]. Since none of the lines passed through the origin, the TC adsorption process adsorbed onto the bioadsorbent was controlled not only by the intra particle diffusion, but also by the boundary layer diffusion. As summarized in Table S1, the calculated $\bf k_{\mathrm{i}}$ and C values abided by $\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{i}1}>\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{i}2}>\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{i}3}$ and $\mathrm{{C}_{\mathrm{{i}1}}<\mathrm{{C}_{\mathrm{{i}2}}<\mathrm{{C}_{\mathrm{{i}3}}}}}$ . This is because at the first stage, there were a large number of effective adsorption sites on the bioadsorbent leading to a quick adsorption behavior. With the adsorption progress, a thick adsorption boundary layer on the outer surface of the bioadsorbent was gradually formed by TC molecules due to the number of active adsorption sites reduced, slowing down the adsorption rate.
## 3.3.4. Adsorption isotherm
Since the TC concentration is one of the major driving forces in the adsorption procedure for the bioadsorbent, the effect of the initial TC concentration on the adsorption behavior was studied. As presented in Fig. 5A, the equilibrium adsorption capacity of bioadsorbent increased gradually with the increase of initial TC concentration at the same temperature. This is due to higher TC concentration resulted in higher driving force which encouraged the TC diffusion into the active sites of bioadsorbent.
Langmuir and Freundlich isotherm models were applied to fit the experimental data in order to illustrate the adsorption mechanism. The Langmuir isotherm model assumes the adsorption is monolayer coverage with homogeneous adsorbent surface. Its isotherm equation is shown in Eq. (7):
$$
\frac{C_{e}}{Q_{e}}=\frac{1}{\mathrm{K_{L}}Q_{m}}+\frac{C_{e}}{Q_{m}}
$$
where $Q_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $\mathsf{Q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ are the maximum and equilibrium adsorption capac­ ities of bioadsorbent $\mathbf{(mg/g)}$ , respectively, ${\sf C}_{\mathrm{e}}$ is the equilibrium TC concentration $(\mathrm{mg/L})$ , $\mathrm{K_{L}}$ is Langmuir constant concerning the
adsorption energy $\left(\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{m}\mathrm{g}\right)$ .
The separation factor [10] is a dimensionless constant presenting the feasibility of any adsorption process which is expressed as follows:
$$
\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{L}}={\frac{1}{1+\mathrm{K}_{L}C_{0}}}
$$
where ${\bf C}_{0}$ is the initial TC concentration, $\mathrm{\mathbf{R}_{L}}$ represents the adsorption to be either irreversible $\mathrm{(R_{L}}<0\rangle$ , liner $\mathrm{(R_{L}=1)}$ ) or unfavorable $\mathrm{(R_{L}>1)}$ ) [43].
Whereas, the Freundlich isotherm model presumes the multilayer adsorption happens on a heterogeneous surface with different effective sites. The Freundlich isotherm equation is listed in Eq. (9):
$$
l o g Q_{e}=l o g\mathrm{K_{F}}+{\frac{1}{n}}l o g C_{e}
$$
where ${\mathrm{K}}_{\mathrm{F}}$ is the Freundlich isotherm constant $\left(\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{m}\mathrm{g}\right)$ and $1/\mathfrak{n}$ presents the adsorption intensity.
The experimental data of TC adsorption onto bioadsorbent were fitted by Langmuir and Freundlich isotherm models, respectively. The plots and the parameters of isotherm models are presented in Fig. 5B-C and Table 2. The Langmuir isotherm model showed a better fit for the TC adsorption due to the higher correlation coefficient $(\mathsf{R}^{2}$ of 0.998) than that of the Freundlich isotherm model (0.982). This suggests the TC adsorption onto the bioadsorbent happened on the homogeneous sur­ face involving monolayer adsorption process. In addition, the $\mathrm{\mathbf{R}_{L}}$ value was calculated to be in the range of $0.249{-}0.768$ , suggesting TC adsorption onto the bioadsorbent was favorable. Based on the Langmuir isotherm model, the theoretical adsorption capacity $\left(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{m}}\right)$ was 1359.2 $\mathbf{mg}/\mathbf{g}$ , which is higher than most reported MOF loaded polysaccharide adsorbents (Table 3). This indicates that the prepared bioadsorbent was a powerful bioadsorbent for TC removal and exhibited a great potential for practical application.
## 3.3.5. Adsorption thermodynamics
The effect of temperature on the equilibrium adsorption capacity was studied and the result in shown in Fig. 5D. It can be clearly observed that the equilibrium adsorption capacity of TC increased with the tempera­ ture increasing. With the temperature increasing from $15^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the TC equilibrium adsorption capacity raised from $155\mathrm{mg/g}$ to $401\mathrm{mg/g},$ demonstrating the TC adsorption process was endothermic. One of the possible reason was the increased temperature could accelerate the mobility of the TC molecules. Another one could be due to the enhanced swelling effect on the inner 3D structure of the bioadsorbent at high temperature that favored the diffusion of TC molecules [53].
The thermodynamics parameters including Gibbs free energy change $\Delta\mathbf{G}^{\circ}_{\mathbf{\Delta}}(\mathbf{kJ}/\mathbf{mol})$ , enthalpy change $\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}(\mathrm{kJ/mol})$ and entropy change $\Delta S^{\circ}$ $\mathrm{(J/(K{\cdot}m o l)}$ were calculated in order to demonstrate the TC adsorption mechanism. They are calculated based on the following equations:
$$
l n\frac{Q_{e}}{C_{e}}=\frac{\Delta S^{\circ}}{R}-\frac{\Delta H^{\circ}}{R T}
$$
$$
\Delta G^{\circ}=\Delta H^{\circ}-T\Delta S^{\circ}
$$
where $\mathtt{R}$ is the gas constant $(8.314\mathrm{~J}/(\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{K}))$ , T is the absolute tem­ perature (K).
As shown in Table 4, $\Delta\mathsf{G}^{\circ}$ values at different temperatures were all negative, demonstrating the TC adsorption onto the bioadsorbent was spontaneous. Besides, $\Delta\mathsf{G}^{\circ}$ value raised with the temperature increasing, confirming the TC adsorption process was exothermic, which is in line with the result of the temperature effect on the TC adsorption that high temperature is beneficial for the adsorption. The calculated positive $\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}$ value confirmed the TC adsorption belonged to an endothermic nature. Generally, $\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}$ below $4.18\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ suggested a physical adsorption, and $\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}$ locating from 80 to $200\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ demonstrated a chemisorption [54]. Since the obtained $\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}$ value was $44.552~\mathrm{{\kJ/mol}}$ , the TC adsorption was considered to be a physic-chemical adsorption process. The positive $\Delta S^{\circ}$ value indicated the randomness degree of TC increased at the solid-liquid interface of the bioadsorbent during the adsorption process [55].
## 3.3.6. Reusability study
The reusability of the bioadsorbent is one of the basic requirements for its practical application. In this study, five adsorption-desorption regeneration experiments were carried out and the removal efficiency of each cycle is listed in Fig. 6. It can be found that the TC removal ef­ ficiency of the bioadsorbent slightly reduced from $83.2\%$ to $65.3\%$ with the increased cycle number in the first three adsorption-desorption cy­ cles and then remained largely the same for the following cycles. Nevertheless, the TC removal efficiency of the bioadsorbent could still reach as high as $63.5~\%$ in 5th cycle. This result suggests the prepared bioadsobent could be regenerated for several times, demonstrating its good reusability for the TC removal.
## 3.4. Proposed adsorption mechanism
The potential mechanism on the adsorption of TC onto Fe/ZIF$8@$ cellulose aerogels was proposed based on the above analysis of the morphology, the chemical structure, the pH effect on adsorption, pseudo-second-order kinetic, intra-particle diffusion and Langmuir isotherm models. Generally, the adsorption process comprises both physical and chemical adsorption [22]. The physisorption relies on the pore-filling mechanism. As the reported studies [1,56], pore filling is considered to be one of the major adsorption mechanisms for the 3D aerogels with porous structure. The porous structure of Fe/ZIF$8@$ cellulose aerogel was confirmed by the SEM, which facilitated the diffusion of TC molecules into the pores through the pore filling effect. Pore filling was also verified through the intra-particle diffusion model. The chemisorption was confirmed by the good fit for the pseudo-secondorder kinetic model. The Langmuir isotherm model suggests the TC adsorption onto the bioadsorbent was a monolayer adsorption process.
FTIR spectrum of TC adsorbed Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose was displayed in
Fig. 7. After TC adsorbed onto Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose, the new absorption peaks appeared at 1449and $1256~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which represents the skeleton vibration of the benzene ring and the ${\tt N}{\mathrm{-}}{\tt H}$ stretching of TC, respectively [57]. This indicates TC was successfully adsorbed onto the Fe/ZIF$8@$ cellulose. The peak shifting from $34\dot{4}6~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to $3410~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ illus­ trates hydrogen bonding formed between the cellulose and TC. The $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathrm{N}$ stretching vibration of imidazole at $1576~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ shifted to 1597 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ after TC adsorption, which demonstrated the presence of $\pi{-}\pi$ in­ teractions between the imidazole ring of Fe/ZIF-8 nanocrystals and the benzene ring of TC. Moreover, the peak locating at $610~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ shifted to $598~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , demonstrating the Fe–O groups involved in the TC binding [58]. Based on the FTIR analysis, the shifts of the -OH stretching and the $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathrm{N}$ stretching of imidazole occurred after TC adsorption, which confirmed the existence of $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions and hydrogen bonding in the TC adsorption process. From the effect of solution pH analysis, the TC adsorption performance displayed a pH dependent manner, suggesting there was electrostatic interaction in the TC adsorption process.
Combining the above results, the mechanism of TC adsorption behavior of Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose aerogels was proposed and shown in Fig. 8. The surface complexation, electrostatic interaction, the hydrogen bonding, and the π-π interaction could synergistically contribute to the ultra-high TC adsorption capacity.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose bioadsorbent was successfully synthesized by in-situ growth of Fe doped MOF strategy. After doping with Fe, the TC adsorption capacity was greatly enhanced. The TC adsorption behavior on Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose bioadsorbent followed by pseudo-second-order kinetics and Langmuir isotherm model with the maximum adsorption capacity of $1359.2\mathrm{mg/g}$ , indicating the properties of chemical adsorption and monolayer adsorption. The outstanding TC adsorption mechanism was proposed based on the synergetic effects including surface complexation, electrostatic attraction, $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction and hydrogen bonding. It is worth noting that the bioadsorbent fabri­ cated in this work presented excellent reusability. These excellent characteristics make Fe/ZIF-8@cellulose aerogel to be a promising bioadsorbent candidate to solve the actual antibiotics pollution problem.
